 deal that you're  quite right the parliament has passed  motions objecting to a no deal breaks it  but it passed a law indeed two laws that  provided us for for us to leave on the  29th of March subsequently delayed and  Lord Trump's motions  the Prime Minister could have taken us  out on the 29th of March to the prime  minister who asked for an extension it  was a prime minister who changed the  date by prerogative power from the 29th  March 12 April there's all rests with  her and upon her shoulders she has made  the Prime Minister mrs. May has made  active choices to stop his leaving and  she deserves to be held to account for  that because people ought to know the  truth of the position rather than trying  to blame everybody else blaming or  counseling MPs and other conservatives  the Prime Minister had done what she'd  said in the first place and had stuck to  the laws set out in two acts we would  have left the European Union by now I  wonder though if actually there is an  argument that conservative brexit is  like yourself do need to shoulder some  of the blame if as many expect the talks  that Jeremy Corbyn lead to a softer  brexit or indeed no breakfast at all I'm  a new consistently whipped opinion  against her reason they still you  described it as risking making Britain a  slave state to the EU of risking paying  39 billion pounds for nothing I mean  it's no surprise she's failed to get the  support when people such as yourselves  have been so opposed to the deal even  though you did have the last-minute  conversion well I've waited for it the  last time out because I thought it was  the least bad option that's absolutely  correct but the least bad option because  of the way decisions are being made by  the government what I said about slave  state and 39 billion for nothing remain  absolutely true that mrs. Mayer's deal  would leave us for 22 hour month subject  to EU law and EU judges without any say  over it at all and paying 39 billion  pounds without any guarantee of a free  trade deal two of the reasons why it's  such a poor deal the greatest reason of  course being the potential to split up  the United Kingdom at the behest of the  EU which is why I said last week that if  we are forced to remain in we should be  the most difficult member possible  because I don't think the EU in its  jargon has behaved towards us with  sincere cooperation i dint think sincere  cooperation could possibly include  trying to break up the unity of a member  state when it leaves the European Union  and therefore I think we no longer  obliged to follow sand circle  operation in return and when the  multi-annual financial framework comes  forward if we're still in this is our  one in seven year opportunity to veto  the budget and to be really very  difficult and I hope that any British  Prime Minister would take that  opportunity can you just spell out I  mean you talked that about vetoing a  budget what exactly do you mean by being  as disruptive as possible well many  things are taken over now by qualified  majority vote and there's nothing we can  do about them in terms of the budget  each annual budget is set by majority  voting but the seven-year budgetary  framework requires unanimity and that we  could veto that we'd also be able to  stop mr. macarons plans for further deep  integration because those would almost  certainly require treaty change so as  they began to progress if we were still  a member we would be able to stop them  so I think it's trying to stop further  integration and trying to ensure that it  doesn't cost us any more money so these  would be things that weren't just  difficult for their own sake but we're  also in the British national interest if  we were still a member state you see  listen to speak it sounds as if we have  an awful lot of power well being a  member of the European Union when I  actually thought that the whole point of  trying to leave was to get sovereignty  we're not I know some people have said  that in response what I said but I don't  think they're looking at the detail and  we can veto the budget one year and  seven for the other years we just have  to take the budget that is delivered  under that framework all our laws on  agriculture on fishing on trade come  from the European Union and we can be  out vated we are one of 28 members that  we can't set our envy 80 system in  detail we can't take v80 of domestic  fuel for example little things like that  that are very important to people's  livelihoods they're set by the European  Union we can't control our borders and  say no no this doesn't undermine the  general view that far too many laws come  from the European Union and that we  don't have democratic control over it  what it does show is that if they keep  us in if they haven't behaved with  sincere cooperation what it sauce for  the goose is sauce for the gander so I  wonder and then we're talking about the  possibility of staying in the European  Union longer  than many people expected are you going  to be voting in the European Parliament  elections or do you think the  Conservative Party should veto them if  they happen oh well if we're still in of  course we should have European elections  that would be quite wrong and it's  fascinating all the people who want to  stay in the European Union rather  dislike democracy they don't much want  the next set of European elections and  they certainly didn't like the  referendum and this part the problem  with the EU that it's a scheme of  imposing things on people rather than on  having proper democratic engagement but  yes if we had those elections I would  vote in them and I would always wait for  a Conservative candidate oh talking  about another election lots of people  are are thinking that it may be that a  general election is around the corner  are you concerned like some of your  colleagues that by inviting Jeremy  Corbyn in to thrash out a grexit  compromise to reason may risks  